;; guest-control.expected.sexp — guest expectation document, converted from guest-control.expected.toml by
;; scripts/convert_dossier.py (SOT-FORMAT.4). One document form; validate with
;;   python3 scripts/check_sexp_schema.py guest-control.expected.sexp schema/expectations.sexp
;; Round-trip: `python3 scripts/convert_dossier.py verify guest-control.expected.toml guest-control.expected.sexp` — data-equal, comment census exact.

(comment "guest-control.expected.toml — expected observations for `guest-control.s`." "" "⛔ DERIVED FROM THE PINNED SPECIFICATION BEFORE THE PROGRAM WAS RUN. Every `derivation` below is" "the reasoning and every `source` is where the rule is stated. An expected value copied out of a" "model's output tests that the model is self-consistent and nothing else." "" "⭐ This program carries NEGATIVE observations as well as positive ones — `never_written`. A jump" "that fails to skip its dead instructions is invisible to a checker that only looks at registers" "it expects to change, so the registers those instructions would have written are named and must" "stay untouched. `docs/CPU_ENVIRONMENT.md` §4 asks for exactly this.")
(expectations (program "guest-control.s") (entry "0x80000000") (instructions 13) (comment "x6 is written only by the two instructions JAL jumps over; x13 only by the one JALR jumps over." "If either appears in the trace, a control transfer silently did not happen.") (never_written "x6") (never_written "x13") (step (n 0) (insn "addi x1, x0, 3") (writes (write (reg "x1") (value "0x0000000000000003"))) (derivation "x0 reads as zero and the 12-bit immediate is sign-extended; 0 + 3 = 3.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.1 (x0 hardwired); §1.1.4")) (step (n 1) (insn "addi x1, x1, -1") (writes (write (reg "x1") (value "0x0000000000000002"))) (derivation "First pass of the loop body.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 2) (insn "bne x1, x0, loop") (writes) (derivation "x1 = 2 != 0, so the branch is TAKEN. A branch writes no register. The offset is -4, added to the address of the BRANCH itself, giving 0x80000004.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.5.2 'The 12-bit B-immediate encodes signed offsets in multiples of 2 bytes … added to the address of the branch instruction'")) (step (n 3) (insn "addi x1, x1, -1") (writes (write (reg "x1") (value "0x0000000000000001"))) (derivation "Second pass — reached only because the branch was taken.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 4) (insn "bne x1, x0, loop") (writes) (derivation "x1 = 1 != 0, taken again.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.5.2")) (step (n 5) (insn "addi x1, x1, -1") (writes (write (reg "x1") (value "0x0000000000000000"))) (derivation "Third pass; the counter reaches zero.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 6) (insn "bne x1, x0, loop") (writes) (derivation "x1 = 0, so BNE is NOT taken and execution falls through. Three executions of one branch instruction with two different outcomes is the point of the loop.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.5.2")) (step (n 7) (insn "jal x5, over") (writes (write (reg "x5") (value "0x0000000080000010"))) (derivation "JAL stores the address of the instruction FOLLOWING the jump (pc+4 = 0x8000000C+4) into rd, then transfers to pc + offset. Note x5 therefore holds the address of an instruction this program never executes.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.5.1 'JAL stores the address of the instruction following the jump (pc+4) into register rd'")) (step (n 8) (insn "addi x7, x0, 7") (writes (write (reg "x7") (value "0x0000000000000007"))) (derivation "At 0x80000018 — proving the jump skipped 0x80000010 and 0x80000014. Control transfers have no architecturally visible delay slot, so nothing between the jump and its target executes.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.5 (no delay slots) — decision D-NO-DELAY-SLOTS")) (step (n 9) (insn "auipc x9, 0") (writes (write (reg "x9") (value "0x000000008000001C"))) (derivation "AUIPC forms a 32-bit offset from the U-immediate with the low 12 bits zeroed, sign-extends it to 64 bits, and adds it to THE ADDRESS OF THE AUIPC INSTRUCTION. With a zero immediate the result is that address itself.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.1 — decision D-LUI-AUIPC")) (step (n 10) (insn "jalr x10, x9, 13") (writes (write (reg "x10") (value "0x0000000080000024"))) (derivation "rd receives pc+4 = 0x80000020+4. The TARGET is x9 + 13 = 0x80000029, an ODD address, whose least-significant bit is then SET TO ZERO — giving 0x80000028. The +13 is deliberately odd so that a model which forgot to clear the bit would land one byte into an instruction and fault.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.5.1 'adding the sign-extended 12-bit I-immediate to the register rs1, then setting the least-significant bit of the result to zero' — decision D-JALR-LSB") (limit "⚠️ HOW FAR THIS ACTUALLY FALSIFIES D-JALR-LSB, measured rather than assumed. Replacing +13 with +12 changes nothing: both land on 0x80000028, BECAUSE the low bit is cleared. So the discriminating observation is not the landing address on its own — it is that execution continues NORMALLY from 0x80000028 instead of the misaligned fetch a model that kept the odd bit would attempt. Both available references clear the bit, so the failing branch of this test has never been observed on any model here, and this is tested evidence for the behaviour rather than a discrimination between two behaviours. Owner of a stronger control: P1-LAB's validator mutation suite, which can mutate OUR model to keep the bit and check that the mutation is caught.")) (step (n 11) (insn "addi x11, x0, 42") (writes (write (reg "x11") (value "0x000000000000002A"))) (derivation "At 0x80000028 — the cleared-LSB target. Reaching THIS instruction rather than 0x80000024 is the observation that D-JALR-LSB holds.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 12) (insn "addi x12, x0, 1") (writes (write (reg "x12") (value "0x0000000000000001"))) (derivation "Falls through; bounds the run at a known instruction count.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")))
